---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CheckerTool from '../components/CheckerTool.tsx';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import { webApplicationLd, proseClass } from './_seo';
const title = 'Check Image Type, KB Size and Pixels | SizeTune';
const description =
  'See what an image really is: its true file type, size in KB and pixel dimensions. Enter your form rules to get a pass or fail, all in your browser.';
const jsonLd = webApplicationLd('SizeTune: check image type, size and pixels', '/check-image-size/', description);
---
<Base pageType="tool" width="wide" title={title} description={description} path="/check-image-size/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Hero title="Will this image pass the form?" lead="Add a picture to see its real file type, size in KB and pixel dimensions. Type in your form's limits and get a pass or fail for each one. Nothing is uploaded." />

  <CheckerTool client:load />
  <noscript>
    <p class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-amber-400 bg-amber-50 p-4">
      This checker reads your file inside your browser, so it will not run while JavaScript is turned off. Enable
      JavaScript and reload to use it. The notes below on why uploads get rejected still apply.
    </p>
  </noscript>

  <div class={`mx-auto max-w-3xl mt-10 ${proseClass}`}>
    <h2>What the checker tells you</h2>
    <p>
      For a picture you choose, the page reads the first part of the file and reports four things: the real file type,
      the size in kilobytes, the width and height in pixels, and the print resolution if the file records one. It does
      this without decoding the whole picture, so it is quick even on a large photo.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you type in the limits from your form, each one gets its own pass or fail line with the number measured from
      your file next to the number the rule asks for. The page has no list of exam or portal rules. You enter what
      your own form says, so the answer matches the form in front of you and not an old copy of someone else's.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the real type matters</h2>
    <p>
      A file name is only a label. Renaming a PNG to end in .jpg changes the label and nothing else, and a picture
      saved from a messaging app can arrive as WebP even though it is called .jpg. Some upload systems look at the
      contents rather than the name, and they reject a file whose contents do not match what the form accepts. The
      checker reads the opening bytes of the file, which identify the format, and warns you when the name and the
      contents disagree.
    </p>
    <p>
      It can also recognise a few formats it cannot measure, such as GIF, PDF, HEIC or SVG. For those it names the type
      so you know what you have, and says that the pixel rules cannot be applied.
    </p>

    <h2>How kilobytes are counted</h2>
    <p>
      A kilobyte is 1,000 bytes on some systems and 1,024 on others. A file of 50,500 bytes is under a 50 KB limit
      counted the second way and over it counted the first way. When your file lands in that gap the checker marks the
      rule as borderline and explains which counting passes, instead of calling it a pass. If the answer matters, make
      the file a little smaller than the limit so it passes under either count.
    </p>

    <h2>A note on resolution</h2>
    <p>
      Some forms mention dots per inch. That number is a label inside the file and does not change how many pixels the
      picture has. The checker shows it only when the file records one in a place it reads: the JFIF header of a JPEG
      or the physical size chunk of a PNG. If it says the resolution is not present, that is exactly what it found, and
      it does not guess a value. Resolution stored in other metadata is not read.
    </p>

    <h2>If a rule fails</h2>
    <p>
      When a size rule fails, the page offers a link to the <a href="/">resizer on the home page</a> with your size
      limit already filled in. The picture itself does not travel between pages, because nothing is stored, so you add it
      again there. Pixel rules are not carried across; set them in the resizer's advanced options. If your picture is
      the wrong way up, <a href="/rotate-image/">turn it first</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Passing here is useful but not a promise. A portal may check a colour mode, a transparent background or the
      width of the margins, none of which this page can see.
    </p>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions about checking an image</h2>
    <h3>Is the file type taken from the name?</h3>
    <p>No. It is read from the first bytes of the file, which is why a renamed file is caught.</p>
    <h3>Why does the page say borderline?</h3>
    <p>
      Your file's size passes if the portal counts 1,000 bytes per kilobyte or 1,024, but not both. Shrink it a little
      to be safe.
    </p>
    <h3>Why is the resolution missing?</h3>
    <p>
      Many files, including most phone photos and screenshots, do not store one in the places the checker reads. It
      shows what is present and never invents a number.
    </p>
    <h3>Can it check a PDF?</h3>
    <p>
      No. It can tell you the file is a PDF, but it only measures JPEG, PNG and WebP pictures. For PDFs, read{' '}
      <a href="/guides/reduce-pdf-to-100kb/">how to get a PDF under 100 KB</a>.
    </p>
    <h3>Is my picture uploaded?</h3>
    <p>
      No. The checking happens in your browser. Read the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> for details.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
